<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 779</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Samye Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samye is the first Buddhist monastery founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, built on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo">Yarlung Tsangpo</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> around 779 under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>, with the Indian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shantarakshita">Shantarakshita</a> and, by tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>. Its plan forms a mandala, with the main temple representing Mount Meru. It was the setting of the debate of the 790s in which Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> prevailed over Chinese Chan teaching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samye.jpg" alt="Samye" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_grand_view_of_Samye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Samye Monastery was built during the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> and is counted as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> and Nyingma monastery. The Testament of Ba gives the earliest dates for its construction, a foundation in a hare year and the consecration of the main shrine in a sheep year, each of which can be read in two ways. All accounts agree that the king was the patron. The first Tibetan monks were ordained there in 779.</p><p>According to the Testament of Ba and later accounts, the Indian scholar Khenpo Śāntarakṣita began the work after accepting the king's invitation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The building kept collapsing, and the workers believed that a demon or obstructive spirit in a nearby river was responsible. On his advice the king invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>, who subdued the obstacles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> described a Vajrakilaya dance and a rite of namkha, followed by the raising of four Vajrakilaya stupas at the cardinal points.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The plan follows a mandala of the Buddhist cosmos. A main temple stands in the middle, representing Mount Meru, enclosed by four stupas of different colours and by a circular wall with four openings at the cardinal points. The three-storeyed main building was designed with its first floor in Indian style, the second in Chinese style and the third in Khotanese style. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the temple is dedicated to Vairocana. A stone pillar of the eighth century records the building of Samye and the oaths of the king, ministers and nobles to protect its endowments, and a bronze bell at the entrance was made by Queen Rgyal mo brtsan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shantarakshita">Śāntarakṣita</a> a dispute among his followers grew into a debate hosted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> in the early 790s. Kamalaśīla spoke for Indian Buddhist theory and Moheyan, a teacher of the East Mountain tradition of Chan, for the Chinese view. The king judged that Kamalaśīla had won.</p><p>The original buildings have long disappeared. Civil war in the 11th century, fires in the mid 17th century and in 1826, an earthquake in 1816 and the Cultural Revolution all damaged the site, and each time it was rebuilt. After 1986 the 10th Panchen Lama, Choekyi Gyaltsen, was the main force behind the latest rebuilding. The Puning Temple at Chengde, built in the 18th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>, was modelled on Samye.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nalanda, Vikramashila, Somapura and Odantapuri attracted monks and students from Tibet, China and Southeast Asia, and Tibetan tradition holds that the monastery of <b>Samye</b>, Tibet’s first, was modelled on Odantapuri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Trisong Detsen invited the Indian scholar Shantarakshita and, by tradition, the tantric master Padmasambhava, founded <b>Samye</b> monastery around 779 and, according to later accounts, presided over a debate around 792–794 in which Indian Buddhism prevailed over Chinese Chan teaching.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tibet received it in the 7th and 8th centuries; the monastery of <b>Samye</b> was founded around 779, and Buddhism later became the foundation of Tibetan culture and government.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
